WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.860
The pursuit of Hollywood success has led many
performers to experience a crisis serious

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.780
enough that they sought professional help
to preserve and strengthen their mental health.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.580
More and more, actors are opening up about
how therapy allowed them to handle some terrifying

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.120
moments in their careers.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.720
Michael B. Jordan's performance as the villainous
Erik "Killmonger" Stevens in the 2018 blockbuster

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.859
Black Panther was so arresting, some critics
felt it overshadowed Chadwick Boseman's lead

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.560
role as King T'Challa of Wakanda, better known
as the Black Panther.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.280
Jordan dove deep to portray Killmonger, T'Challa's
militant, long-lost cousin, but it seems his

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.620
star turn as the charismatic anti-hero came
at a price.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.640
Jordan explained in an interview with Bill
Simmons that he had never spent as much time

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.040
inhabiting a character who was, quote, "that
dark, that lonely, that painful."

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.520
"All this death… just so I can kill you."

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.460
The actor found it hard to let those feelings
go.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.460
He told Simmons,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.390
"Once I got finished wrapping the movie, it
took me some time to talk through how I was

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.900
feeling, and why I was feeling so sad, and
like a little bit depressed."

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.290
Psychotherapist Robin Halpern is familiar
with this phenomenon, and explained that the

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.900
intensity of these same roles can lead to
difficulty in separating the actor's feelings

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.680
form the character.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.720
She explained that this is especially difficult
when the characters are dealing with something

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.990
that the actors themselves are sensitive to,
with a performance that taps into their own

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:25.980
feelings.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:27.680
"Burn it all!"

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.880
Indeed, Jordan later said,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.280
"[Killmonger is] an extreme, exaggerated version
of the African diaspora from the African-American

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.710
perspective, so to be able to take that kind
of pain and rage and all those emotions that

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.890
Erik kind of represents from being black and
brown here in America […] that was something

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.280
I didn't take lightly."

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.560
When Jordan realized he was still carrying
Killmonger's pain and isolation into his personal

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.520
life, he sought out professional help, and
while he acknowledged the cultural stigma

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.050
that men in particular face about mental health
treatment, he also spoke about how it truly

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:58.580
helped him.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.320
Since his debut in Captain America: The First
Avenger, Chris Evans has embodied the never-say-die

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.920
attitude and moral center of the Marvel Universe's
Steve Rogers, but he almost didn't take the

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:09.420
role.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.960
When talking about how he dealt with mental
illness, Evans discussed his anxiety, which

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:17.940
was so intense that it actually led him to
turn down the role of Captain America when

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:18.960
it was initially offered.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.500
Instead of embracing a deal for six films,
a massive commitment that is surely the dream

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.740
of most working actors, Evans was obsessively
focused on his fears.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:34.280
"And if I'm the only one, then so be it…
but I'm willing to bet I'm not."

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.600
Afraid of losing his anonymity and being unable
to cope with the demands of fame amidst a

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.130
role that would span ten years of his life,
Evans turned to his therapist for advice,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:44.740
saying,

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.380
"I asked every human being in my life what
they thought, and they said I should do the

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.220
movie, and then I went to therapy."

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.520
Sure enough, after working through his anxiety
with a professional, he was able to come to

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.660
a realization that taking the risk might be
worth it.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.580
Evans is thankful that therapy enabled him
to take on the role that made him famous,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.760
but he's also grateful that his fear pushed
him to seek help, and even said that if he

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.780
hadn't taken the role of Captain America,
he might have never sought help for his anxiety.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.920
Jared Padalecki has spent well over a decade
hunting angels, demons, and too many other

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.920
paranormal entities to list as Sam Winchester
in Supernatural.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.900
However, none of those fictional battles,
or the fame that came with them, prepared

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.440
him for a struggle with depression.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.930
While shooting a Christmas episode for the
show's third season, Padalecki was overcome

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:33.080
by a depressive episode so serious he found
himself unable to move.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.880
"I walked into my trailer, sat down on the
couch, and I couldn't get up.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.100
I could no longer, on my own, muster the will
to carry on."

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.040
Costar Jensen Ackles took one look at Padalecki
and knew his TV brother needed some help,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.080
so he called the on-set doctor, who diagnosed
Padalecki with clinical depression.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.460
Instead of seeking treatment, Padalecki only
took a few days off from filming, both because

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.060
he was concerned that any longer breaks would
negatively affect the livelihoods of the show's

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.960
crew, and also because of his difficulty in
admitting his own troubles.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.280
It took until just before the start of Supernatural's
11th season for him to break down.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.220
During a stopover in Geneva on his way to
some convention appearances, he found himself

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.960
sobbing uncontrollably in a public park, deeply
depressed.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.500
A timely phone call from a friend convinced
Padalecki to make it home to his family, where

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.640
he finally was able to take a break and get
the help that he needed.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:26.600
In the end, Padalecki applied the same logic
to mental health that he did to physical health.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:30.800
Reasoning that if he wanted to get in peak
physical shape he'd employ a personal trainer,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.960
he knew he needed to do something similar
to get in shape mentally.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.420
He's carried those lessons forward, starting
the "Always Keep Fighting" campaign to raise

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.300
funds for a charity that aims to give help
to people struggling with depression, addiction,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.020
self-injury, and suicide.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.500
The CW's Crazy Ex-Girlfriend has broken barriers
in its depiction of mental health issues,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.940
showing compassion for the difficulties of
its central character as she grapples with

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.880
the negative behaviors associated with her
eventual diagnosis of borderline personality

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.380
disorder.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.220
"Just a couple questions …how did I get
BBD?

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:03.230
Is BBD curable?

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:04.230
How serious is BBD?

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.230
Is BBD easy to fix?

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:06.230
Is BBD genetic?

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.080
Did I get BBD from my mother?

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:07.920
I got it from my mother right?

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.840
That tone is something that Rachel Bloom,
co-creator and star of the series, prioritized

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.040
along with her co-creator, Aline Brosh McKenna.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.900
In the kind of synchronicity that you'd expect
from a scripted series, it was the process

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.910
of pitching Crazy Ex-Girlfriend that led Bloom
to be kinder to herself and seek out a psychiatrist

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.240
for the first time.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.380
Her anxiety had led to insomnia so intense
that Bloom began to become delirious, leading

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.460
her to be afraid that her lack of sleep would
ruin her chances of getting what would ultimately

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.980
become a very personal show picked up by the
network.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.020
That, in turn, led her to a psychiatrist,
and to work that was genuinely transformative.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:50.500
"Yeah the verdict's in I am officially medically
certifiably quote unquote … crazy."

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.360
Bloom would later say,

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.900
"In his office I finally felt safe.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:56.020
[…] I told him everything.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.820
Each session improved my life."

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.460
It was also educational.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.700
While she was initially reluctant to use medication
to help treat her mental illness, she had

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.460
fears that they were something to be ashamed
of, or a sign of weakness.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.500
After seeking treatment herself, she admitted
that those first feelings were wrong, which

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.800
led to a musical number in Crazy Ex-Girlfriend's
fourth season, appropriately titled "Antidepressants

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.340
Are So Not a Big Deal."

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:24.360
When Kit Harrington won the part of Jon Snow
on Game of Thrones in 2010, he couldn't have

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.500
known that the show would be a massive success,
or the level of scrutiny it would bring to

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.000
his life.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.680
A relative unknown with only a few admittedly
impressive theater credits, Harrington was

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.900
thrust into the spotlight that would follow
him for the next decade.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.220
As Jon Snow found himself at the center of
the battle to rule over the fictional kingdom

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.720
of Westeros, pressure mounted on the young
actor.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:48.220
When Harrington broke his foot after a night
of drinking before the start of season three,

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:53.260
John Bradley, who plays Snow's friend Samwell
Tarly, told Rolling Stone that Harrington's

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:56.900
serious approach to his craft left him down
about disappointing his coworkers.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:01.960
In 2017, Harrington discussed his character's
death and resurrection with the New York Times,

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.640
and the interviewer noted that it seemed Jon
Snow hadn't yet come to terms with the strange

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:07.240
situation.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.820
Harrington replied that while our world acknowledges
that opening up and solving problems through

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:16.000
therapy is a very positive thing, that's not
really an option for the King in the North

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.180
in his world of dire wolves and dragons.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.800
Even if it was, it seems doubtful that the
character would've pursued it.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.580
As Harrington put it, Jon Snow isn't exactly
comfortable opening up.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:28.800
"I don't want it.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.860
I never have."

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:35.360
Fortunately, that's not a trait that Harrington
shares with his character, and he later revealed

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.060
that he was seeking treatment himself.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.620
Following Jon's rebirth, Harrington was struggling
with feelings of vulnerability and doubts

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.720
about his own talent, even despite his success,
and that led him to therapy.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:47.740
Child star.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:48.380
Teen idol.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:49.280
Talk show host.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:49.880
Peacock.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.060
Donny Osmond has been all these things and
many more in a career that has spanned over

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:54.900
five decades.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.900
His version of the song "Puppy Love" topped
the Billboard music charts in 1972, and he'd

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.100
be back at the top again with "Soldier of
Love" in 1989.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.340
In 2008, he began an 11-year residency in
Las Vegas, performing with his sister Marie

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:08.960
Osmond.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:14.440
In 2019, he found himself in the spotlight
in a new way, as the runner-up finalist in

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.660
the Masked Singer competition, in which he
was disguised in a full-body costume to compete

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.120
against other masked celebrity singers.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:25.280
It wasn't the first time Osmond had donned
a colorful costume to sing his heart out.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:30.960
In 1999, he starred in a film adaption of
Joseph and the Amazing Technicolor Dreamcoat.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.640
The role came during a difficult point in
his life, when the pressure to deliver the

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.220
kind of performance that would cement his
adult career was fierce.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:43.020
In an interview in 2000, he spoke about the
intensity that would lead to a diagnosis of

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:44.960
social anxiety disorder, saying

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.640
"I thought I was actually going crazy in my
mind.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.620
I remember shaking in bed, and I just couldn't
get out of bed.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.220
Something was wrong, and my wife took me to
the hospital."

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:57.500
The film's producers brought in a therapist
to work with the star, who taught Osmond to

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.540
engage with his concerns rather than trying
to ignore or get away from them.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.820
Osmond's work in therapy helped him to cope
with the fear he still fights against today.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.780
In 2013, Marvel Studios announced that Chris
Pratt would star in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:15.140
The deal made a leading man out of Pratt,
but triggered a period of depression that

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.220
would ultimately threaten the life of another
actor who had been considered for the part:

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:20.420
Zachary Levi.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.860
Speaking to Men's Journal, the eventual star
of 2019's Shazam! recalled that losing the

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.960
role to Pratt was a blow to his self-worth,
and one of the factors that, quote, "drove

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:30.840
me into darkness."

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.360
Levi had struggled with self-esteem since
childhood, rooted in an upbringing that saw

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.270
him and his sisters suffering the effects
of being raised by a mother who had herself

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.560
been abused psychologically throughout her
life.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:46.260
Levi continued to pursue his career, but his
mental health continued to suffer.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:53.060
In 2016, he flew to Australia to reprise his
role as Fandral in Thor: Ragnarok, only to

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:55.580
see most of his work end up on the cutting
room floor.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.860
It didn't help that around the same time,
his brief marriage imploded and his mother

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:00.360
died.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:01.700
He would later say that,

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.440
"I felt very beat up by Hollywood.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:07.460
I was at a place in my life where I didn't
really understand why I wanted to live anymore."

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.780
Instead of giving in to the pain of depression,
Levi reached out for help.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:17.060
He sent himself to a three-week mental health
retreat in Connecticut, where he saw a psychotherapist

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:21.220
and a behavioral therapist in addition to
learning meditation techniques, therapy that

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.060
he said, quote, "saved my life."

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.740
It was there he received word he'd been called
back for Shazam!.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.960
He filmed his audition on his phone from the
retreat, A week after completing the program,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.480
Levi was being fitted for his Shazam costume.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:38.360
If you or anyone you know is having suicidal
thoughts, please call or chat online with

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:44.260
the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline at
1-800-273-TALK (8255).

